From: unjordi <jordi@ciencias.unam.mx>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: qcom_battmgr: wake only on a real charger
 attach/detach

With the battmgr supplies registered without a wakeup source (0903),
routine battery telemetry no longer wakes the AP -- but a charger
attach or detach must still wake it long enough to service the
charge-start handshake before it re-suspends. On QCS8550/kalama-class
parts the charger is handled entirely in the ADSP and the AP has no
dedicated charger IRQ; the only wake path is the glink doorbell, and
ucsi_glink in this tree carries no wakeup logic of its own, so the
battmgr client must provide it.

A first version of this patch armed a wakeup event on every
NOTIF_USB_PROPERTY/NOTIF_WLS_PROPERTY notification (the charger
notification class, as opposed to NOTIF_BAT_PROPERTY's routine
telemetry). That was too coarse: on-device testing at mid-SOC with an
active PD charge showed the ADSP pushes NOTIF_USB_PROPERTY repeatedly
while charging (current/voltage/type telemetry rides the same
notification class as an actual plug event, not just NOTIF_BAT_PROPERTY),
so every one of those re-armed a hard wakeup and the AP never managed
to re-enter suspend -- a wake-storm through the USB door instead of the
transport-level one 0903/this series already closed.

Replace the blanket wake with one gated on whether usb.online/
wireless.online actually changed. qcom_battmgr_notification() runs on
the glink RX path with channel->recv_lock held (qcom_glink_rx_data() in
qcom_glink_native.c calls the rpmsg callback under that spinlock), so it
cannot itself block on the firmware round trip needed to read a fresh
online value (qcom_battmgr_request() sleeps on a completion). Defer that
off the RX path instead: the notification handler schedules a work item,
which re-queries ONLINE via the same qcom_battmgr_usb_sm8350_update()/
qcom_battmgr_wls_sm8350_update() calls sysfs already uses, and arms a
short hard pm_wakeup_dev_event() only when the value flipped from what
was last armed. Routine telemetry notifications resolve to "unchanged"
and arm nothing; a genuine attach/detach flips the cached value and
wakes the AP once. If the off-path query itself fails (firmware busy or
timed out), fall back to waking unconditionally rather than risk
silently swallowing a real transition.

Deferring the decision opens a missed-wakeup race: between the handler
scheduling the work and the work running, autosleep/s2idle/deep can
complete suspend and freeze the workqueue, so the work never decides and
the attach is lost. Arming the wakeup inside the work is too late -- if
the system already suspended there is nobody left to wake. Instead the
handler takes a wakeup-source hold (pm_stay_awake()) before scheduling
and the work drops it (pm_relax()) on every exit path; the hold keeps
suspend from completing until the decision is made. pm_stay_awake() and
pm_wakeup_dev_event() both take the wakeup source's lock with
spin_lock_irqsave(), so they are safe to call from the RX callback under
channel->recv_lock. The hold is balanced against schedule_work()
coalescing (one hold per pending run, one pm_relax() per run) so an idle
system with the cable in still suspends. This is the same client-side
wakeup idiom upstream took for ucsi_glink (device_init_wakeup() +
pm_wakeup_dev_event(hard=true) on the connector event), extended with
the pm_stay_awake()/pm_relax() hold because -- unlike ucsi_glink, whose
event is already an edge-like connector notification -- battmgr cannot
confirm the transition synchronously on the RX path and so must defer.
The works run on the unfreezable system_wq: a freezable queue would
freeze exactly when the work must run to decide whether to abort the
suspend.

SC8280XP/X1E80100 don't have an equivalent per-property USB/WLS fetch --
they derive usb.online/wireless.online from the battery status response
instead -- so there's no off-path query to reuse for them here; they
keep the unconditional wake on every USB/WLS notification.

The device is made wakeup-capable with devm_device_init_wakeup() rather
than a bare device_init_wakeup(dev, true): qcom_battmgr is tristate
(CONFIG_BATTERY_QCOM_BATTMGR=m on these boards), and the plain call
leaves the wakeup source enabled after the driver unbinds. The devm
variant registers a teardown action so the wakeup source is released on
unbind/module removal, matching the all-devm resource handling already
used by this probe. Its return is checked (it can fail allocating the
devm action) and surfaced through dev_err_probe(), like the neighbouring
registrations. devm_device_init_wakeup() is available since Linux 6.15
and is present in this tree's 7.2.3 base.

Because battmgr is tristate and the works sleep on the firmware, a
notification landing just before unbind could leave a work dereferencing
the devm-freed battmgr. Register a devm action that cancel_work_sync()s
both works; it is registered before devm_pmic_glink_client_alloc() so
devm's LIFO teardown deregisters the glink client first (stopping new
notifications), then cancels the works, then tears down the wakeup
source -- so a final pm_relax() from a cancelled-but-pending work still
has a live source.

Signed-off-by: unjordi <jordi@ciencias.unam.mx>
---
 drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c | 171 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 171 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
--- a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/nvmem-consumer.h>
 #include <linux/of_device.h>
+#include <linux/pm_wakeup.h>
 #include <linux/power_supply.h>
 #include <linux/property.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/pdr.h>
@@ -34,6 +35,13 @@ enum qcom_battmgr_variant {
 #define NOTIF_BAT_PROPERTY		0x30
 #define NOTIF_USB_PROPERTY		0x32
 #define NOTIF_WLS_PROPERTY		0x34
+
+/*
+ * Hold a wakeup source briefly across a charger attach/detach so the AP
+ * stays up long enough to service the charge-start handshake before it
+ * re-enters suspend.
+ */
+#define QCOM_BATTMGR_WAKEUP_MS		50
 #define NOTIF_BAT_STATUS		0x80
 #define NOTIF_BAT_INFO			0x81
 #define NOTIF_BAT_CHARGING_STATE	0x83
@@ -333,6 +341,16 @@ struct qcom_battmgr {
 
 	struct work_struct enable_work;
 
+	/*
+	 * Deferred off the glink RX path (see qcom_battmgr_notification()):
+	 * re-queries USB/WLS online state and arms a wakeup only when it
+	 * actually toggled, instead of on every USB/WLS notification.
+	 */
+	struct work_struct usb_online_work;
+	struct work_struct wls_online_work;
+	bool usb_online_wake_armed;
+	bool wls_online_wake_armed;
+
 	/*
 	 * @lock is used to prevent concurrent power supply requests to the
 	 * firmware, as it then stops responding.
@@ -1190,6 +1208,113 @@ static const struct power_supply_desc sm8350_wls_psy_desc = {
 	.get_property = qcom_battmgr_wls_get_property,
 };
 
+/*
+ * qcom_battmgr_notification() runs on the glink RX path with
+ * channel->recv_lock held (see qcom_glink_rx_data() in
+ * qcom_glink_native.c), so it cannot block. Resolving usb.online/
+ * wireless.online needs a firmware round trip (qcom_battmgr_request(),
+ * which sleeps on a completion), so that has to happen off-path. These
+ * two work items do that: re-query the property from process context,
+ * and arm a wakeup only when it actually flipped -- not on every
+ * USB/WLS notification, which is what caused the wake-storm this
+ * replaces (routine PD/telemetry doorbells while charging at mid-SOC
+ * were each arming a hard wakeup event; the ADSP keeps charging
+ * regardless of whether the AP stays up for each one).
+ *
+ * SC8280XP/X1E80100 derive usb.online/wireless.online from the battery
+ * status response instead of a dedicated USB/WLS property fetch, so
+ * there is no equivalent off-path query for them here; keep waking on
+ * every notification on that variant rather than guess.
+ */
+static void qcom_battmgr_usb_online_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct qcom_battmgr *battmgr = container_of(work, struct qcom_battmgr,
+						     usb_online_work);
+	bool online;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (battmgr->variant == QCOM_BATTMGR_SC8280XP ||
+	    battmgr->variant == QCOM_BATTMGR_X1E80100) {
+		pm_wakeup_dev_event(battmgr->dev, QCOM_BATTMGR_WAKEUP_MS, true);
+		goto relax;
+	}
+
+	ret = qcom_battmgr_usb_sm8350_update(battmgr, POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_ONLINE);
+	if (ret) {
+		/*
+		 * Couldn't confirm the state off-path (firmware busy or
+		 * timed out); wake conservatively rather than risk silently
+		 * missing a real attach/detach.
+		 */
+		pm_wakeup_dev_event(battmgr->dev, QCOM_BATTMGR_WAKEUP_MS, true);
+		goto relax;
+	}
+
+	online = battmgr->usb.online;
+	if (online != battmgr->usb_online_wake_armed) {
+		battmgr->usb_online_wake_armed = online;
+		pm_wakeup_dev_event(battmgr->dev, QCOM_BATTMGR_WAKEUP_MS, true);
+	}
+relax:
+	/*
+	 * Release the deferral hold taken by the notification handler for
+	 * this run. Every exit path must reach this (hence goto, not return):
+	 * a missed pm_relax() leaks the hold and the AP never suspends again.
+	 */
+	pm_relax(battmgr->dev);
+}
+
+static void qcom_battmgr_wls_online_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct qcom_battmgr *battmgr = container_of(work, struct qcom_battmgr,
+						     wls_online_work);
+	bool online;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (battmgr->variant == QCOM_BATTMGR_SC8280XP ||
+	    battmgr->variant == QCOM_BATTMGR_X1E80100) {
+		pm_wakeup_dev_event(battmgr->dev, QCOM_BATTMGR_WAKEUP_MS, true);
+		goto relax;
+	}
+
+	ret = qcom_battmgr_wls_sm8350_update(battmgr, POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_ONLINE);
+	if (ret) {
+		pm_wakeup_dev_event(battmgr->dev, QCOM_BATTMGR_WAKEUP_MS, true);
+		goto relax;
+	}
+
+	online = battmgr->wireless.online;
+	if (online != battmgr->wls_online_wake_armed) {
+		battmgr->wls_online_wake_armed = online;
+		pm_wakeup_dev_event(battmgr->dev, QCOM_BATTMGR_WAKEUP_MS, true);
+	}
+relax:
+	/* See qcom_battmgr_usb_online_work(): release the deferral hold. */
+	pm_relax(battmgr->dev);
+}
+
+static void qcom_battmgr_cancel_online_works(void *data)
+{
+	struct qcom_battmgr *battmgr = data;
+
+	/*
+	 * Registered before devm_pmic_glink_client_alloc(), so devm's LIFO
+	 * teardown deregisters the glink client first: by the time this runs
+	 * no further notification can queue these works. Drain whatever the
+	 * last one left in flight before battmgr is freed (it is tristate;
+	 * a work still touching battmgr after unbind would be a UAF). A work
+	 * still pending (cancelled before it ran) never reached its pm_relax,
+	 * leaving the notification handler's pm_stay_awake() unbalanced;
+	 * cancel_work_sync() reports that case, so relax it to keep the count
+	 * even while the wakeup source is still alive (its own devm teardown
+	 * runs after this one).
+	 */
+	if (cancel_work_sync(&battmgr->usb_online_work))
+		pm_relax(battmgr->dev);
+	if (cancel_work_sync(&battmgr->wls_online_work))
+		pm_relax(battmgr->dev);
+}
+
 static void qcom_battmgr_notification(struct qcom_battmgr *battmgr,
 				      const struct qcom_battmgr_message *msg,
 				      int len)
@@ -1214,9 +1339,30 @@ static void qcom_battmgr_notification(struct qcom_battmgr *battmgr,
 		power_supply_changed(battmgr->bat_psy);
 		break;
 	case NOTIF_USB_PROPERTY:
+		/*
+		 * Keep the AP awake across the deferred decision: autosleep/
+		 * s2idle/deep must not complete suspend (which freezes the
+		 * workqueue) before usb_online_work runs and decides whether
+		 * this was a real attach. pm_stay_awake() is spinlock-safe
+		 * (spin_lock_irqsave), so it is fine on this RX path (this
+		 * callback runs under the glink channel->recv_lock).
+		 *
+		 * Balance against schedule_work() coalescing: hold once per
+		 * *pending* work run. If the work was already queued this
+		 * notification did not add a run, so its pending run will
+		 * pm_relax() the earlier hold -- drop this one to stay
+		 * balanced (invariant: outstanding holds == pending work runs,
+		 * each run relaxes exactly one).
+		 */
+		pm_stay_awake(battmgr->dev);
+		if (!schedule_work(&battmgr->usb_online_work))
+			pm_relax(battmgr->dev);
 		power_supply_changed(battmgr->usb_psy);
 		break;
 	case NOTIF_WLS_PROPERTY:
+		pm_stay_awake(battmgr->dev);
+		if (!schedule_work(&battmgr->wls_online_work))
+			pm_relax(battmgr->dev);
 		power_supply_changed(battmgr->wls_psy);
 		break;
 	default:
@@ -1667,6 +1813,8 @@ static int qcom_battmgr_probe(struct auxiliary_device *adev,
 	psy_cfg_supply.no_wakeup_source = true;
 
 	INIT_WORK(&battmgr->enable_work, qcom_battmgr_enable_worker);
+	INIT_WORK(&battmgr->usb_online_work, qcom_battmgr_usb_online_work);
+	INIT_WORK(&battmgr->wls_online_work, qcom_battmgr_wls_online_work);
 	mutex_init(&battmgr->lock);
 	init_completion(&battmgr->ack);
 
@@ -1737,6 +1885,29 @@ static int qcom_battmgr_probe(struct auxiliary_device *adev,
 					     "failed to register wireless charing power supply\n");
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Wake the AP on a charger attach/detach (the USB/WLS notifications
+	 * handled above); the routine battery telemetry is left to the
+	 * supplies, which are registered without a wakeup source. Use the
+	 * devm variant so the wakeup source is torn down automatically when
+	 * the module is unbound (battmgr is tristate).
+	 */
+	ret = devm_device_init_wakeup(dev);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "failed to enable wakeup\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * Cancel the online works on unbind. Registered before the glink
+	 * client below so devm's LIFO teardown deregisters the client (and
+	 * thus stops new notifications) before this cancels the works,
+	 * and before the wakeup source is torn down, so any final pm_relax
+	 * still has a live source.
+	 */
+	ret = devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, qcom_battmgr_cancel_online_works,
+				       battmgr);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
 	battmgr->client = devm_pmic_glink_client_alloc(dev, PMIC_GLINK_OWNER_BATTMGR,
 						       qcom_battmgr_callback,
 						       qcom_battmgr_pdr_notify,
